Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?feat(api):=20S1=20=E2=80=94=20/api/v1=20foundat?=
 =?UTF-8?q?ion=20(login,=20logout,=20me,=20branding)?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The first mobile API endpoints. Everything downstream (S6 student API, S8
instructor API) depends on this shape existing before it multiplies across
60+ endpoints.

Auth is email + password -> Sanctum bearer token, NOT the OTP flow the
implementation plan originally assumed. spims-edu's actual login
(AuthService::login) has always been password-based; OTP is used only for
email verification and password reset. Corrected before implementing rather
than building the wrong contract:

  POST /api/v1/login  { email, password, device_name? } -> { token, user }
  POST /api/v1/logout                                    -> 204, revokes only this token
  GET  /api/v1/me                                        -> the caller's profile + roles
  GET  /api/v1/branding                                  -> public, safe pre-login

AuthService gains issueApiToken()/revokeApiToken(), sharing the existing
verifyCredentials() check (wrong password, suspended, not-yet-active) with the
web login rather than forking it. issueApiToken() deliberately does NOT call
Auth::login() - that needs a started session, which the stateless api
middleware group does not provide; conflating the two paths would have made
API login silently depend on session middleware that was never applied to it.

Tokens carry role abilities (role:STUDENT, role:INSTRUCTOR, ...) so later
phases can gate mutations without re-touching login.

config/auth.php gains the 'sanctum' guard. It was missing entirely - auth:sanctum
would have thrown 'Auth guard [sanctum] is not defined' for every request,
including the pre-existing /api/user scaffold route, which no test exercises
and was therefore never known to be broken.

One error shape for every /api/v1 failure: { message, code, errors? }.
AuthorizationException defines its own render(), which Laravel checks before
any Handler::renderable() callback runs - a callback registered in Handler
would never fire for it, so it is special-cased directly instead. A 404 from a
missing route-bound model no longer leaks the model's FQCN in its message.

Locale (Accept-Language -> user's preferred_locale -> en) is resolved directly
at the point each translated string is produced, not only via a middleware
side-effect: Laravel's global middleware-priority list runs auth checks before
ordinary route middleware regardless of registration order, so an
unauthenticated request's 401 could fire before SetApiLocale ever ran. Caught
by LocaleTest failing against the middleware-only version during development.

No new migration - purely additive app/routes/config/lang/docs, verified
against PostgreSQL 16 as well as the in-memory SQLite test suite.

docs/api/openapi.yaml is hand-maintained; OpenApiCoverageTest fails the build
if a registered api/v1 route has no matching entry.

--- Independent review (separate agent, cold review of this diff) ---

Verified against framework/Sanctum source, three of this commit's own
technical claims:
- AuthorizationException::render() precedence over Handler renderable
  callbacks: Handler.php:455-457 runs it before renderViaCallbacks at 464-466.
- Middleware-priority reordering auth ahead of SetApiLocale regardless of
  registration order: Kernel.php's $middlewarePriority + SortedMiddleware's
  interface/parent-class matching, confirmed empirically by reverting the fix
  and watching LocaleTest fail exactly as predicted.
- Sanctum RequestGuard's per-instance user-caching requiring Auth::forgetGuards()
  between requests in one test method: RequestGuard.php:52-59 + AuthManager's
  guard memoization, confirmed by removing the workaround and watching two
  tests false-pass 200 instead of 401.

Found and fixed:
- codeForStatus() had no 401/403 mapping. Harmless for this app's own
  AuthorizationException (hardcoded to FORBIDDEN, never reaches this method),
  but Illuminate\Auth\Access\AuthorizationException - the type Policies,
  Gate::authorize(), and can: middleware throw - has no render() of its own,
  gets converted to AccessDeniedHttpException, and DOES reach this method;
  without the mapping it fell to the generic 'ERROR' code. Two exception types,
  identical HTTP status, different code - the exact inconsistency 'one error
  shape' exists to prevent, the moment either is actually used.
- OpenApiCoverageTest filtered on route NAME ('api.v1.' prefix) rather than
  URI. A route registered under /api/v1/* with an unrelated name - a typo, or
  one added outside the Route::prefix('v1')->name('api.v1.') group - passed
  the coverage test while being live, reachable, and undocumented. Reproduced
  exactly as described, then fixed to filter on $route->uri() instead; the
  same reproduction now fails the test.
- Added a regression test locking in that the 'api' middleware group carries
  no session middleware, which is the actual reason Sanctum's
  session fallback can never authenticate an /api/v1 request today - and would
  silently stop being true if EnsureFrontendRequestsAreStateful or StartSession
  were ever added to that group.
- Added a test locking in the zero-role-account edge case (reachable via
  UserAdminService::createUser() with an empty roles array): login succeeds,
  the token's abilities array is empty, and it therefore fails closed on every
  future tokenCan() check - confirmed deliberately, not left unspecified.
- Fixed a stale docblock on ApiError.php contradicting ApiLocale.php's own
  documented reasoning.

Deliberately NOT fixed here: AuthorizeService::authorize(null, ...) throws
this app's own AuthorizationException (403-only render()) for what is
semantically a 401 case (message text is the unauthenticated string, wrapped
in a FORBIDDEN envelope). This is pre-existing behavior in code S0 shipped
untouched by this commit - existing coverage
(tests/Feature/Auth/AuthorizeServiceTest::guest_raises_unauthorized) asserts
only the exception type, not its rendered status, so the mismatch was never
caught. Not reachable via any S1 route today (none call AuthorizeService).
Changing it means changing what AuthorizeService/AuthorizationException throw
for a null actor, which has its own blast radius and belongs in its own
dedicated, test-first patch - not folded into an API-foundation commit as a
side effect of review.

38 tests across 6 files (up from 34 after the review pass), verified failing
against the pre-fix code where a fix was made. 176 total passing (up from
139), 712 assertions.
